{"name":"Hosta Flower","hex":"#dcdde7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hosta","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several herbaceous Asiatic plants of the genus Hosta."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hosta"},{"term":"flower","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colorful, conspicuous structure associated with angiosperms, frequently scented and attracting various insects, and which may or may not be used for sexual reproduction.","A reproductive structure in angiosperms (flowering plants), often conspicuously colourful and typically including sepals, petals, and either or both stamens and/or a pistil."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To put forth blooms.","To decorate with pictures of flowers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flower"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hosta","blocks":[{"text":"Hosta is a genus of plants commonly known as hostas, plantain lilies and occasionally by the Japanese name gibōshi. Hostas are widely cultivated as shade-tolerant foliage plants. The genus is placed in the family Asparagaceae, subfamily Agavoideae, and is native to northeast Asia. Like many \"lilioid monocots\", the genus was once classified in the Liliaceae. The genus was named by Austrian botanist Leopold Trattinnick in 1812, in honor of the Austrian botanist Nicholas Thomas Host. In 1817, the generic name Funkia was used by German botanist Kurt Sprengel in honor of Heinrich Christian Funck, a collector of ferns and alpines. It was later used as a common name and is referenced in some older literature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Hosta_Bressingham_Blue.JPG/330px-Hosta_Bressingham_Blue.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hosta","wikidata":"Q623347"},{"title":"Flower","blocks":[{"text":"Flowers, also known as blossoms and blooms, are the reproductive structures of flowering plants. Typically, they are structured in four circular levels around the end of a stalk. These include: sepals, which are modified leaves that support the flower; petals, often designed to attract pollinators; male stamens, where pollen is presented; and female gynoecia, where pollen is received and its movement is facilitated to the egg. When flowers are arranged in a group, they are known collectively as an inflorescence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg/330px-Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower","wikidata":"Q506"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hosta sieboldiana by Abraham Jacobus Wendel, 1868","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAfbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Hosta_capitata2UME.jpg/330px-Hosta_capitata2UME.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Hosta_capitata2UME.jpg/330px-Hosta_capitata2UME.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHosta_capitata2UME.